WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.020 --> 00:00:05.819
As the second of Ridley Scott's Alien prequels,
Alien: Covenant picks up on the themes we

00:00:05.819 --> 00:00:09.680
saw in Prometheus and continues to build a
backstory for the Xenomorph we first met in

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:11.860
Scott's 1979 Alien movie.

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:15.980
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here
and in this video, I want to take a look at

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:20.450
some of the easter eggs and details in Alien:
Covenant that only die-hard fans will have

00:00:20.450 --> 00:00:21.450
spotted.

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:24.620
Do let me know about your favourite easter
eggs or any others you spotted in the comments

00:00:24.620 --> 00:00:29.110
below and also subscribe for a chance to win
this awesome Alien: Covenant merchandise.

00:00:29.110 --> 00:00:33.810
I've got two packs to give away and each one
includes a T-shirt, a cap, a keychain bottle

00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:36.850
opener, metal playing cards and a set of pin
badges.

00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:41.890
Obviously, there are spoilers ahead for Alien:
Covenant, so be aware of that or come back

00:00:41.890 --> 00:00:43.610
after you've seen the movie.

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:47.880
When the Covenant crew land on the planet,
two of the team are infected with Neomorph

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:52.460
spores after they disturb the local flora
causing the spores to be released into the

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:54.149
air and enter their ear or nostrils.

00:00:54.149 --> 00:00:59.149
This airborne means of infection is actually
a call-back to a rejected script for Alien

00:00:59.149 --> 00:01:01.140
III by writer William Gibson.

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:04.920
Gibson's story, which would have been the
sequel to director James Cameron's Aliens

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:09.639
movie, explored the theme of genetically altering
Xenomorphs into bioweapons.

00:01:09.639 --> 00:01:14.020
And this idea also links to David's experiments
in Alien: Covenant and how he combined the

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:19.049
lethal "black liquid" pathogen with DNA from
the Engineers and Elizabeth Shaw to produce

00:01:19.049 --> 00:01:22.329
his own experimental alien creations.

00:01:22.329 --> 00:01:26.179
During the confrontation between David and
Walter, David quotes one of the most famous

00:01:26.179 --> 00:01:30.840
lines from John Milton's epic poem Paradise
Lost, in which Satan declares, "Better to

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:33.299
reign in Hell than serve in Heaven."

00:01:33.299 --> 00:01:37.180
There's a parallel between David's story in
Alien: Covenant and Prometheus and how he

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:41.729
turned against the humans he served, and the
themes of Milton's Paradise Lost which tells

00:01:41.729 --> 00:01:46.029
the story of the fallen angel Lucifer, and
how he corrupted mankind in the Garden of

00:01:46.029 --> 00:01:49.890
Eden, after he was banished to Hell when he
rebelled against God.

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:54.069
By the way, "Paradise Lost" was the original
title for the Prometheus sequel until it was

00:01:54.069 --> 00:01:55.369
changed to "Covenant".

00:01:55.369 --> 00:01:59.899
And that title change is interesting because
in the poem "Paradise Lost", Adam experiences

00:01:59.899 --> 00:02:04.659
a vision of future events right up until the
Biblical Great Flood, which ties in with the

00:02:04.659 --> 00:02:09.160
idea of the covenant God made with Noah who
built the ark that saved humanity and the

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:10.350
animals.

00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:13.920
And just like the Biblical story of the ark
and the Great Flood where each species came

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:17.490
in twos, did you notice how all the crew on
the Covenant are couples?

00:02:17.490 --> 00:02:22.100
And, at the end of the movie, David himself
also brought a pair of alien embryos onto

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:24.070
the Covenant ship.

00:02:24.070 --> 00:02:28.130
And the seed of David's rebellion against
humankind was planted during that opening

00:02:28.130 --> 00:02:32.820
scene in Alien: Covenant where we see the
first moments of David's creation and self-awareness

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:34.380
with Peter Weyland.

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:39.340
In that scene in Weyland's room, David names
himself after the statue of Michelangelo's

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:43.460
David, likely because he felt that looking
at the statue of David was, in some ways,

00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:48.320
like looking in a mirror, in other words,
both synthetic and statue were perfect creations

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:51.680
that would long outlive their human creators.

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:55.350
Intriguingly though, the choice of David and
Walter for the names of the two synthetics

00:02:55.350 --> 00:03:00.380
in Alien: Covenant is actually an homage to
the producers of all the Alien movies.

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:04.460
Alien: Covenant features the drinking bird
toy that has appeared in several of the Alien

00:03:04.460 --> 00:03:07.480
movies, starting with Ridley Scott's 1979
film.

00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:11.270
I'm curious as to whether this is just an
easter egg or whether there's a deeper meaning

00:03:11.270 --> 00:03:14.460
to the repeated placement of the toy bird
on various ships.

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:17.060
Perhaps it's just a fun souvenir from home.

00:03:17.060 --> 00:03:20.020
Or could it suggest the monotony of their
lives on board?

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:24.620
Or maybe the reason it's there is hinted at
in Prometheus where a programmed message plays

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:27.710
after the crew wake up, encouraging them to
drink enough water.

00:03:27.710 --> 00:03:32.430
Along with the original Alien movie, one of
Ridley Scott's best-known sci-fi films is

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:37.010
1982's Blade Runner starring Harrison Ford,
and there are a number of easter eggs and

00:03:37.010 --> 00:03:39.990
possible links between Blade Runner and Alien:
Covenant.

00:03:39.990 --> 00:03:44.470
For example, this emergency signal which appears
in Blade Runner also appears in Covenant as

00:03:44.470 --> 00:03:46.080
well as Prometheus.

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:49.830
And one of the scenes in Alien: Covenant tips
its hat to a scene in Blade Runner.

00:03:49.830 --> 00:03:54.400
When Daniels uses a nail to stab David, he
mocks her saying "that's the spirit!"

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:59.010
In Blade Runner, Batty impales his own hand
with a nail and moments later taunts Deckard

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:04.020
as they fight with the line "that's the spirit!"
Ridley Scott is also a producer on the sequel,

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:09.600
Blade Runner 2049, and that movie's trailer
teases a suspended humanoid figure which bears

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:12.990
some similarity to the Engineers from the
Alien movies.

00:04:12.990 --> 00:04:17.070
With both films coming out this year and apparent
easter eggs and references between the two

00:04:17.070 --> 00:04:20.650
movies, could this be Ridley Scott hinting
at a possible shared universe?

00:04:20.650 --> 00:04:25.419
Talking of crossovers, there's a nod to Predator
in the scene on the Covenant where the alien

00:04:25.419 --> 00:04:27.490
chases Daniels and Tennessee through the ship.

00:04:27.490 --> 00:04:31.820
We get a point-of-view shot for the alien as

00:04:31.820 --> 00:04:34.520
it hunts the remaining pair of crew members.

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:38.840
But the Predator franchise is no stranger
to this type of POV shot, and of course, that

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:43.050
franchise crossed over with the Alien franchise
in the Alien vs Predator movies.

